do $seed$
declare cid uuid;
begin
  select id into cid from public.courses where slug = 'bib-102';
  insert into public.course_items (course_id, kind, position, title, content) select cid, 'topic', 21, $j$6.1 Revelation: John on Patmos and the Seven Churches$j$, $j${"readings": [{"title": "Revelation 1 (King James Version)", "url": "/portal/bible/Revelation/1"}, {"title": "Revelation 2 (King James Version)", "url": "/portal/bible/Revelation/2"}, {"title": "Revelation 3 (King James Version)", "url": "/portal/bible/Revelation/3"}], "body": "Read Revelation 1, Revelation 2 and Revelation 3 in the King James Version. Then read this lesson.\n\nRevelation is the last book of the Bible. It was written by the apostle John near the end of the first century, when he was an old man and was on the island of Patmos because of his witness for Jesus. The book begins with a vision of the risen Christ, who says that He is the first and the last, that He is alive for evermore, and that He holds the keys of hell and of death. Jesus then sent seven letters to seven churches in Asia Minor. He praised what was good, corrected what was wrong, and called each church to repent and to overcome. To the church at Ephesus He said, Thou hast left thy first love. To Smyrna He said, Be thou faithful unto death, and I will give thee a crown of life. To Laodicea He said, Behold, I stand at the door, and knock.\n\nKey words: revelation, Patmos, first love, faithful, overcome.\n\nYour task: (1) Tell who wrote Revelation and where he was. (2) Write Revelation 1:18 in your own words. (3) Choose one of the seven churches and tell what Jesus said to it.\n\nCourse area: Bible elective. Scripture is read from the King James Version.", "video": "", "presenter": "Dr. Alex L. Jenkins Sr.", "standards": "Bible elective", "script": "Welcome to Unit 6, our last unit. The book of Revelation is the final book of the Bible. Many people think it is only about frightening events, but the first verse tells us what the book is really about. Revelation 1:1 says, The Revelation of Jesus Christ, which God gave unto him, to shew unto his servants things which must shortly come to pass; and he sent and signified it by his angel unto his servant John: This is a revelation of Jesus Christ. It shows us who He is and how the story ends. Verse 3 adds a blessing. Blessed is he that readeth, and they that hear the words of this prophecy, and keep those things which are written therein: for the time is at hand.\n\nThe writer is John, the same apostle who wrote the Gospel and the three letters. He was now an old man. Revelation 1:9 says, I John, who also am your brother, and companion in tribulation, and in the kingdom and patience of Jesus Christ, was in the isle that is called Patmos, for the word of God, and for the testimony of Jesus Christ. Patmos is a small rocky island in the Aegean Sea, where John had been sent because he would not stop preaching about Jesus.\n\nOn that island John saw a vision of the risen Lord. He was so overcome that he fell at His feet. Revelation 1:17 and 18 says, And when I saw him, I fell at his feet as dead. And he laid his right hand upon me, saying unto me, Fear not; I am the first and the last: I am he that liveth, and was dead; and, behold, I am alive for evermore, Amen; and have the keys of hell and of death. Jesus is alive, and He holds the keys. Earlier in verse 8 He said, I am Alpha and Omega, the beginning and the ending, saith the Lord, which is, and which was, and which is to come, the Almighty. Alpha and Omega are the first and last letters of the Greek alphabet. Jesus is the beginning and the end.\n\nIn chapters 2 and 3 Jesus sent seven short letters to seven churches in Asia Minor. These were real churches, and each letter speaks to their real condition. To Ephesus, a hard working church, He said in Revelation 2:4, Nevertheless I have somewhat against thee, because thou hast left thy first love. Hard work cannot replace love for Jesus. To Smyrna, a suffering church, He said in Revelation 2:10, be thou faithful unto death, and I will give thee a crown of life. To Laodicea, a comfortable church, He said in Revelation 3:16, So then because thou art lukewarm, and neither cold nor hot, I will spue thee out of my mouth. Yet He spoke with love. Revelation 3:19 says, As many as I love, I rebuke and chasten: be zealous therefore, and repent. Then came one of the most tender invitations in the Bible. Revelation 3:20 says, Behold, I stand at the door, and knock: if any man hear my voice, and open the door, I will come in to him, and will sup with him, and he with me. Jesus does not force the door open. He knocks, and He waits for us to open.\n\nEach letter ends with a promise to the one who overcomes. Revelation 3:21 says, To him that overcometh will I grant to sit with me in my throne, even as I also overcame, and am set down with my Father in his throne.\n\nYour task is to tell who wrote Revelation and where he was, write Revelation 1:18 in your own words, and choose one of the seven churches and tell what Jesus said to it. Then take the four question check.\n\nJesus is alive, He holds the keys, and He is knocking at the door. Excellence, Faith, Purpose. I will see you in the next lesson."}$j$::jsonb where not exists (select 1 from public.course_items where course_id = cid and kind = 'topic' and title = $j$6.1 Revelation: John on Patmos and the Seven Churches$j$);
end
$seed$;
